#---
# purpose: "forty live structs, every schema id residue mod 16 among them, each holding 8 in its first field, survive collections unchanged: a struct is forwarded only by its own marker word, never because its header and first field look like one"
#---
#
# A struct's schema id is assigned in registration order, so forty distinct
# struct types cover every low nibble a schema id can have, including 8. Each
# instance stores 8 as its first field. A collector that read "header low
# nibble 8 and second word 8" as an already-moved object would take the struct
# whose id ends in 8 for a forwarded one. `make` keeps all forty live while
# `main` builds 20000 list cells, so collections run, then prints them.
defmodule R0 do
  defstruct [:a, :b]
  @type t :: %R0{a: integer, b: integer}
end
defmodule R1 do
  defstruct [:a, :b]
  @type t :: %R1{a: integer, b: integer}
end
